Test mechanics: scripts write `$!` of the backgrounded subshell to a pidfile so tests assert liveness with `kill(pid, 0)` under bounded polling (≤3 s, 20 ms steps; a killed grandchild is reparented to init/launchd and reaped, so `ESRCH` is the terminal state). Marker absence is asserted after the pid is gone and again after a short bounded wait past the child's scheduled write time. No sleeps longer than ~1 s in any test; all scripts are `#!/bin/sh`, skipped on Windows like the existing tests.

## Invariants

- I1: On Unix, every ordinary descendant remaining in the command's process group receives SIGKILL before `subproc.Run` returns, on every return path (success, non-zero exit, deadline, cancel, WaitDelay abort).
- I2: Pipe draining stays bounded by `WaitDelay`; a group kill on cancel typically makes `Wait` return sooner than today because the pipe holders die.
- I3: Timeout precedence is unchanged: callers still test `ctx.Err()` before `runErr`; `os.ErrProcessDone` from `Cancel` on an already-exited group never surfaces as a caller-visible error.
- I4: No delayed timer or background goroutine touches a pgid after `Run` returns (pgid reuse safety).
- I5: Interactive cancel still works: SIGINT/SIGTERM to etude cancels the root context, which cancels the group. Second signal → default action.
- I6: Env handling, `Dir`, stream buffers and the reserved-env skip logic at each site are byte-for-byte unchanged apart from the two replaced lines.

## Edge cases and known limitations (disclosed for adjudication)

- Not a sandbox: a child that calls `setsid`/`setpgid` leaves the group and is not reached. Documented, out of scope.
- Zombies: a killed descendant may linger as a zombie until its parent/init reaps it. We guarantee termination was delivered, not disappearance. Tests poll to `ESRCH`.
- pgid reuse after `Wait`: `cleanup` runs immediately after `Wait` reaps the direct child. If the whole group had already exited, the pid could in principle have been reused by a new process that also made itself a group leader in that microsecond window. The window is bounded to the synchronous cleanup call (no timer), which is the trade-off the ticket explicitly accepts. A pgid still in use cannot be reused (POSIX), so the guarantee for surviving descendants is unaffected.
- `EPERM` from `kill(-pgid)` (a descendant changed uid): fall back to killing the direct child; surfaced only via the existing error path.
- `Setpgid` moves the child out of the terminal foreground group. Stdin is `/dev/null` (Cmd.Stdin nil) and stdout/stderr are pipes or `/dev/null` at all four sites, so no SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU stops occur. A seat adapter that opens `/dev/tty` directly would be affected; none does.
- `exec.Start` returns an error for a non-`CommandContext` cmd with `Cancel` set; the helper documents the contract and a test pins the loud failure.
- Windows/other: `configure`/`cleanup` are no-ops; existing direct-child kill + `WaitDelay` remains. Compiles via `GOOS=windows go build ./...`.
- Concurrency: seats/checks may run concurrently; each command has its own group, no shared state in `subproc`. `go test -race` on the five packages.

